1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Majestic in their simplicity.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:06,000
Awe-inspiring in their size.

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:12,000
They've housed every imaginable flight vehicle created.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Now, hangers on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,320
Shelter. Refuge. Sanctuary. We seek these comforts to fight another day.

6
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:49,400
For more than a century, hangars, or sheds as they're known in Europe,

7
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:54,040
have provided the same sense of security for all flight vehicles.

8
00:00:54,041 --> 00:01:04,480
the larger the aircraft the larger the hangar today nowhere does that principle resonate more

9
00:01:04,481 --> 00:01:12,160
than in brand germany about 60 miles south of berlin and now home to the largest hangar ever

10
00:01:12,161 --> 00:01:19,480
built imagine a single edifice large enough to completely enclose the louisiana superdome

11
00:01:19,481 --> 00:01:24,480
Cargo Lifter's new mammoth hangar is such a beast.

12
00:01:24,481 --> 00:01:32,640
The cargo lifter shed by Brandt is an absolute humongous structure that overshadows anything

13
00:01:32,641 --> 00:01:37,520
that we've seen to date, including anything that was ever built in the United States.

14
00:01:37,521 --> 00:01:49,160
This hangar is roughly 1,200 feet long, 700 feet wide, and 350 feet tall.

15
00:01:49,161 --> 00:01:57,280
Without any support walls, it is the largest freestanding building in the world.

16
00:01:57,281 --> 00:02:02,780
This hangar will be home to the immense CL-160 airship.

17
00:02:02,781 --> 00:02:08,780
The CL will be 853 feet long and nearly 200 feet wide.

18
00:02:08,781 --> 00:02:16,120
It will be the biggest airship ever built, more than 50 feet longer than the Hindenburg.

19
00:02:16,121 --> 00:02:20,720
Construction began in March 1999 on an empty Russian military airfield.

20
00:02:21,860 --> 00:02:24,920
It took nearly one and a half million cubic feet of concrete

21
00:02:24,921 --> 00:02:29,580
just to secure the foundations for the five overarching steel girders

22
00:02:29,581 --> 00:02:31,560
that make up the hangar's frame.

23
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:38,700
The girders themselves are comprised of 14,000 tons of steel apiece.

24
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:46,100
The only way to secure the three-layer membrane that stretches between each of the arches

25
00:02:46,101 --> 00:02:52,380
was for workers to hang from ropes attached to sections of the frame several hundred feet above

26
00:02:52,381 --> 00:02:54,032
the ground.

27
00:02:54,033 --> 00:03:07,100
The doors, which resemble a clamshell, slide underneath each other, exposing the hangar

28
00:03:07,101 --> 00:03:08,784
to the outside world.

29
00:03:08,785 --> 00:03:15,040
They create a 600-foot-wide, 300-foot-high semicircular opening at each end of

30
00:03:15,041 --> 00:03:16,125
the hangar.

31
00:03:16,126 --> 00:03:23,720
The structure is large enough to house 14 Boeing 747s, the largest commercial

32
00:03:23,721 --> 00:03:25,904
aircraft in the world.

33
00:03:25,905 --> 00:03:32,160
Cargo Lifter wants to have two airships in its hangar, but have

34
00:03:32,161 --> 00:03:34,487
only one in the air at any time.

35
00:03:34,488 --> 00:03:38,340
They are risking millions on this colossal structure.

36
00:03:38,341 --> 00:03:43,340
They're betting a market exists for heavy cargo transport airships.

37
00:03:43,341 --> 00:03:51,340
They plan to carry payloads up to 160 tons and are targeting freight manufacturers,

38
00:03:51,341 --> 00:03:54,340
drilling equipment and heavy machinery companies.

39
00:03:54,341 --> 00:03:57,340
It shows that they're very serious about building airships.

40
00:03:57,341 --> 00:04:03,340
A lot of people propose to build airships, but they forget about building the hangar first.

41
00:04:03,341 --> 00:04:09,660
Now that its hangar is finished, CargoLifter is moving quickly on plans for its mammoth

42
00:04:09,661 --> 00:04:11,580
airship.

43
00:04:11,581 --> 00:04:18,060
Filled with helium and powered by helicopter engines, the CL160 will be so large that a

44
00:04:18,061 --> 00:04:23,020
two-bedroom house could sit comfortably on its tail fin.

45
00:04:23,021 --> 00:04:28,220
CargoLifter hopes to have airships flying by the end of the decade.

46
00:04:28,221 --> 00:04:33,340
These dramatic efforts signal Germany's re-emergence as the industry's leader in size and quality

47
00:04:33,341 --> 00:04:36,820
of airships and the cathedrals that house them.

48
00:04:37,940 --> 00:04:44,380
I would say that we are witnessing a remarkable revival in interest in lighter than air.

49
00:04:44,820 --> 00:04:48,300
Almost 60,000 individuals have invested in the cargo lifter.

50
00:04:48,301 --> 00:04:53,560
There are a lot of people willing to take a certain amount of financial risk, if you

51
00:04:53,561 --> 00:04:59,180
will, in seeing if it's feasible to build an airship of that size.

52
00:04:59,181 --> 00:05:05,180
All airships, cargo lifters included, have been distinguished by their great size.

53
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:08,900
Accordingly, their dwellings have been just as massive.

54
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:17,300
In the late 1880s, hot air balloons were housed in hangars and used to take passengers on short flights.

55
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:25,815
But German Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin, who rode in a hot air balloon as an observer during the U.S.

56
00:05:25,816 --> 00:05:27,760
Civil War, had grander plans.

57
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:31,240
He wanted to build massive, lighter-than-air vehicles.

58
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:37,820
And he said, what would happen if I took a series of round balloons and I put them all together?

59
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:41,180
And that would form an elongated balloon.

60
00:05:42,580 --> 00:05:45,200
Von Zeppelin envisioned putting a frame around his balloons.

61
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:49,540
This would give his craft stability and greater carrying capacity.

62
00:05:49,541 --> 00:05:55,680
He would be able to travel greater distances, increasing the chance for his grand plan of commercial passenger service.

63
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:03,120
But before he could build his new airship, the 416-foot-long LZ-1,

64
00:06:04,020 --> 00:06:07,300
von Zeppelin needed to build the world's largest airship hangar.

65
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:15,580
The 490-foot-long, 52-foot-wide and 52-foot-tall hangar

66
00:06:15,581 --> 00:06:17,900
was a technological marvel in 1900.

67
00:06:19,060 --> 00:06:21,220
The wooden structure was built on water,

68
00:06:22,020 --> 00:06:24,460
sparing von Zeppelin the need to buy expensive land.

69
00:06:25,420 --> 00:06:27,960
To stabilize the shed's outer shell and floor,

70
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:30,940
workers secured a 41-ton anchor

71
00:06:30,941 --> 00:06:33,220
to the bottom of Germany's Lake Constance,

72
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:35,340
about one-half mile from the shore.

73
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:39,580
For six months, more than 100 men

74
00:06:39,581 --> 00:06:41,180
constructed the hangar on the lake,

75
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:43,020
towing wood out to the site.

76
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:48,440
The hangar itself is built on pontoons,

77
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:51,080
anchored on the one end with no door on that side.

78
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:53,940
On the back side was simply an opening

79
00:06:53,941 --> 00:06:57,340
in which a second pontoon, actually it's a pontoon floor,

80
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:03,300
could leave the entire shed with the airship attached to the floor.

81
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:06,080
Now freed from the hangar,

82
00:07:06,480 --> 00:07:09,020
the airship was lofted skyward by the ground crew.

83
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:14,560
The LZ-1, like all airships, had to take off directly into the wind.

84
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:18,440
This gave the ground crew more control of the airship.

85
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:21,520
And that's what he very cleverly did

86
00:07:21,521 --> 00:07:24,820
when he towed this floating hangar out on Lake Constance

87
00:07:24,821 --> 00:07:27,840
and was able to launch his airships there.

88
00:07:29,620 --> 00:07:31,220
After completing its mission,

89
00:07:31,340 --> 00:07:33,140
the airship would return to the platform,

90
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:34,960
be tied down,

91
00:07:35,460 --> 00:07:37,160
and then with the help of tugboats,

92
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:39,020
be pushed back into the hangar.

93
00:07:41,460 --> 00:07:43,240
Von Zeppelin quickly moved forward

94
00:07:43,241 --> 00:07:46,300
and began building more airships and more hangars.

95
00:07:46,301 --> 00:07:52,960
In fact, Germany had erected 22 airship hangars by the start of World War I.

96
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:59,800
But von Zeppelin's doubleshed in Friedrichshafen, the first in the world, was his crown jewel.

97
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:03,840
It could accommodate two airships at the same time.

98
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:12,800
The mammoth facility was a staggering 603 feet long, 150 feet wide, and 82 feet high.

99
00:08:12,801 --> 00:08:22,118
During the wintertime, the airships were usually hung up in a shed so that the structure not only had to take the weight of snow from the winter months, but also had

100
00:08:22,119 --> 00:08:28,500
to take the dead weight of an airship, or in this case in Friedrichshafen, two airships hanging from the ceiling.

101
00:08:30,580 --> 00:08:36,920
Despite the success of the double shed in Friedrichshafen, hangars didn't gain wide acceptance until World War I.

102
00:08:38,440 --> 00:08:41,900
As the war progressed, airships were used as bombers.

103
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:50,920
and both single and double sheds were built close to the front lines throughout German occupied

104
00:08:50,921 --> 00:08:57,300
territories however along with the benefits of having lots of airships close to each other

105
00:08:57,301 --> 00:09:05,280
came the pitfalls one of these was the Germans use of hydrogen the highly explosive gas was

106
00:09:05,281 --> 00:09:11,600
directly responsible for several accidents that occurred at hangar bases the more famous location

107
00:09:11,601 --> 00:09:12,960
was a place called Alhorn.

108
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:16,900
Alhorn had three sets, that is, in all,

109
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:18,880
six double shed standing there.

110
00:09:19,300 --> 00:09:21,260
The problem with this arrangement was that

111
00:09:21,261 --> 00:09:23,800
the hangars at Alhorn were parallel to each other,

112
00:09:23,860 --> 00:09:25,780
and they were constructed too close to one another.

113
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:29,080
As one of the airships was being repaired,

114
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:30,860
it caught fire and exploded,

115
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:33,820
causing debris to land on its neighboring shed,

116
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:35,800
which had two airships inside.

117
00:09:36,860 --> 00:09:38,420
A chain reaction occurred

118
00:09:38,421 --> 00:09:44,660
in which all four airship sheds that were sitting next to one another blew up.

119
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:50,560
The Germans lost, in one mishap, five airships, all of the more recent designs,

120
00:09:50,620 --> 00:09:52,760
so it was the latest in technology, if you will,

121
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:57,640
and four of their brand new airship hangars that they had just built.

122
00:09:57,860 --> 00:10:00,760
And these were the largest ones that had been built at that time.

123
00:10:00,761 --> 00:10:07,400
They were 260 meters in length and 60 meters wide and, I believe, 50 meters high.

124
00:10:08,420 --> 00:10:13,580
With the sudden loss of these hangars and the great hydrogen-filled ships' vulnerability in the war,

125
00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:17,020
the German military began rethinking its reliance on airships.

126
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:19,520
But this proved to be a moot point.

127
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:22,280
Following its loss in World War I,

128
00:10:23,120 --> 00:10:27,600
Germany was forbidden from building airships and their hangars by the Treaty of Versailles.

129
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:31,940
However, the rest of the world desired airships,

130
00:10:31,941 --> 00:10:36,660
and following the war, the popularity of airships and hangars grew.

131
00:10:38,420 --> 00:10:45,420
Next, America follows Germany's lead and builds the biggest airships and hangars in the world.

132
00:10:45,421 --> 00:10:57,420
Von Zeppelin's floating hangar on Lake Constance was one of over 80 hangars Germany was forced to destroy as a result of the Treaty of Versailles.

133
00:10:57,421 --> 00:11:03,420
Hangars will return on Modern Marvels.

134
00:11:03,421 --> 00:11:10,215
During World War I, Germany's effective use of airships had impressed U.S.

135
00:11:10,216 --> 00:11:11,960
military officials.

136
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:16,904
In fact, immediately after the war, the U.S.

137
00:11:16,905 --> 00:11:20,960
began searching for places to house and launch their own airships.

138
00:11:22,660 --> 00:11:28,800
In May 1919, the U.S. Navy purchased 1,700 acres of land in Lakehurst, New Jersey,

139
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:32,340
with the intent to build America's first airship field.

140
00:11:33,420 --> 00:11:37,620
Only six months later, ground was broken on what would become Hangar 1,

141
00:11:38,380 --> 00:11:39,700
Lakehurst Naval Air Station.

142
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:42,300
This hangar was going to be very special,

143
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:44,200
because not only would it house airships,

144
00:11:44,300 --> 00:11:48,280
it would be used for the construction of the Navy's first large rigid airship,

145
00:11:48,660 --> 00:11:49,820
the USS Shenandoah.

146
00:11:50,900 --> 00:11:53,720
Hangar 1 was designed much like the German hangars,

147
00:11:53,721 --> 00:11:56,220
but on a significantly larger scale.

148
00:11:57,080 --> 00:12:00,320
It was built with Germany's hydrogen-filled airship fleet in mind.

149
00:12:00,321 --> 00:12:08,060
At this time, helium wasn't used in airships, so engineers were forced to deal with hydrogen's explosive nature.

150
00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:11,980
With Germany's Allhorn disaster still fresh in their minds,

151
00:12:12,840 --> 00:12:16,880
American designers were determined to build this hangar with an eye toward safety.

152
00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:23,640
Inside hangar number one, all the wiring, all the piping, everything was encased in gas-tight fittings.

153
00:12:23,880 --> 00:12:30,020
The switch rooms, all types of telephone connections, everything was gas-proof, explosion-proof.

154
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:33,300
The sheathing on the outside of the building was originally asbestos

155
00:12:33,301 --> 00:12:35,780
to cut down any damage from fire or sparks.

156
00:12:38,220 --> 00:12:42,200
In 1921, two years after construction began,

157
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:46,200
the mammoth hangar was completed at a cost of $4 million.

158
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:54,080
At nearly 1,000 feet long, 350 feet wide, and 224 feet high,

159
00:12:54,580 --> 00:12:56,640
Hangar 1 was a jaw-dropping creation.

160
00:12:56,641 --> 00:13:04,460
but its four leaf sliding counterbalance doors were the most impressive feature of this cathedral

161
00:13:04,461 --> 00:13:10,447
they each weighed 1,350 tons as much as a world war one u.s.

162
00:13:10,448 --> 00:13:13,840
navy destroyer and the doors could

163
00:13:13,841 --> 00:13:21,680
be opened mechanically or manually using a capstan these doors are kind of unique they roll outward

164
00:13:21,681 --> 00:13:22,840
on railroad tracks.

165
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:26,120
It takes about 10 minutes to open and close the doors,

166
00:13:26,121 --> 00:13:29,700
and they were so well built that the doors are still openable today.

167
00:13:30,480 --> 00:13:33,280
The only drawback to these sliding horizontal doors

168
00:13:33,281 --> 00:13:36,220
was that they projected beyond the sides of the hangar

169
00:13:36,221 --> 00:13:39,120
and created disturbances due to crosswinds.

170
00:13:41,360 --> 00:13:43,400
Wind is the greatest cause of problems

171
00:13:43,401 --> 00:13:46,120
when docking and undocking any airships.

172
00:13:46,121 --> 00:13:53,170
Nevertheless, Hangar 1 was the only structure large enough to accommodate the U.S.

173
00:13:53,171 --> 00:13:53,871
Naval

174
00:13:53,872 --> 00:13:54,600
airships.

175
00:13:54,601 --> 00:14:03,260
The USS Shenandoah was 680 feet long and 79 feet in diameter.

176
00:14:03,261 --> 00:14:08,320
She cruised along at a robust 60 miles per hour.

177
00:14:08,321 --> 00:14:14,800
By 1924, she shared Hangar 1 with the USS Los Angeles, a German-built airship given to

178
00:14:14,801 --> 00:14:18,324
the U.S. Navy as part of World War I reparations.

179
00:14:18,325 --> 00:14:21,920
Now home to the two largest airships in the world,

180
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:26,780
the public was awed by Lakehurst. Well, it was one of the wonders of the world,

181
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:31,620
and although it didn't remain the longest or largest airship hangar for long, it still

182
00:14:31,621 --> 00:14:33,302
continued to amaze people.

183
00:14:33,303 --> 00:14:37,700
Even into the 1930s and 40s, sailors that were stationed here remark

184
00:14:37,701 --> 00:14:43,640
about how they would get off the train, and then there was nothing but this huge, enormous hangar

185
00:14:43,641 --> 00:14:45,560
in the middle of this big, empty field,

186
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:47,500
and Hangar 1 dominated the landscape

187
00:14:47,501 --> 00:14:48,820
pretty much as it does today.

188
00:14:51,700 --> 00:14:54,480
The U.S. Navy's success in using airships

189
00:14:54,481 --> 00:14:56,720
along the East Coast spurred more hangars

190
00:14:56,721 --> 00:14:58,700
and airships to be built further west.

191
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:01,480
All that was needed for the hangar

192
00:15:01,481 --> 00:15:03,000
was lots of empty land.

193
00:15:04,180 --> 00:15:07,600
In January 1929, the Goodyear Company

194
00:15:07,601 --> 00:15:09,900
started construction of a hangar in Akron, Ohio

195
00:15:09,901 --> 00:15:13,140
that would house the largest U.S. Navy airships ever built.

196
00:15:13,640 --> 00:15:19,880
More than 1,300,000 cubic yards of earth,

197
00:15:20,340 --> 00:15:22,740
enough to fill 400 Olympic-sized swimming pools,

198
00:15:22,860 --> 00:15:24,580
was moved from former farmland

199
00:15:24,581 --> 00:15:28,180
to construct the world's largest airship hangar.

200
00:15:29,180 --> 00:15:32,560
Five sets of railroad tracks, totaling 11 miles,

201
00:15:33,160 --> 00:15:35,380
supported railroad derricks used in construction.

202
00:15:36,640 --> 00:15:39,620
Workers drove 1,300 steel pilings

203
00:15:39,621 --> 00:15:41,860
approximately 25 feet into bedrock

204
00:15:41,861 --> 00:15:46,460
for the foundation of the airship hangar known as the Akron Air Dock.

205
00:15:48,020 --> 00:15:53,360
The girders or the supports for the sidewalls were fabricated on the ground.

206
00:15:53,780 --> 00:15:57,780
Then one side was put in place and the second one was put in place.

207
00:15:58,320 --> 00:16:05,840
And then to hold the top portion in shape, there were temporary columns that were erected on each one.

208
00:16:05,841 --> 00:16:11,640
and then a locomotive crane would pick up that top section,

209
00:16:12,160 --> 00:16:13,480
hoist it up into position,

210
00:16:13,900 --> 00:16:18,920
and then lower it down until the side girders and the top were in place.

211
00:16:19,840 --> 00:16:23,620
Similar to a Roman arch, the air dock's strength came from its shape.

212
00:16:25,100 --> 00:16:28,840
Once the middle or keystone section was put into place, the arch was complete.

213
00:16:30,040 --> 00:16:33,100
This evenly transferred the weight of the arch to each side

214
00:16:33,101 --> 00:16:35,280
while pushing the sides together.

215
00:16:35,840 --> 00:16:37,440
which strengthened the entire structure.

216
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:43,040
The new air dock would reflect lessons learned from Lakehurst.

217
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:46,800
The most important of these was door design.

218
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:52,680
Unlike the Lakehurst doors, which opened beyond the hangar, causing wind disturbances,

219
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:59,520
the air dock used an innovative design, the clamshell, also known as orange peel doors.

220
00:16:59,521 --> 00:17:06,280
The doors were set on a railroad track and when opened would slide around the shell of the hangar.

221
00:17:08,880 --> 00:17:11,480
The air dock only took 12 months to complete.

222
00:17:12,520 --> 00:17:20,540
When finished, it measured 1,175 feet long, 325 feet wide, and 211 feet tall.

223
00:17:22,980 --> 00:17:29,420
In June 1929, in the new air dock, engineers started work on the first of two great airships.

224
00:17:29,520 --> 00:17:33,060
the 785-foot-long USS Akron.

225
00:17:34,920 --> 00:17:37,700
They completed it in September 1931.

226
00:17:40,580 --> 00:17:44,760
Immediately afterward, work started on the similarly-sized USS Macon.

227
00:17:45,920 --> 00:17:48,860
The Macon stayed in Akron only long enough to be built.

228
00:17:50,060 --> 00:17:54,900
Its permanent home would be about 2,000 miles west of Akron in Northern California.

229
00:17:55,900 --> 00:18:00,140
The U.S. Navy considered 70 locations before building an air station,

230
00:18:00,260 --> 00:18:04,120
including an airship hangar, in Sunnyvale, a San Jose suburb.

231
00:18:05,300 --> 00:18:07,540
There was no television in those days,

232
00:18:07,620 --> 00:18:11,220
so this took place of watching your favorite program on TV.

233
00:18:11,480 --> 00:18:14,340
Just go down there to Mountain View, Sunnyvale area,

234
00:18:14,341 --> 00:18:16,020
and watch Hangar 1 being built.

235
00:18:17,380 --> 00:18:20,600
It took nearly $5 million to turn a hayfield

236
00:18:20,601 --> 00:18:23,420
into the West Coast's largest airship hangar.

237
00:18:24,900 --> 00:18:38,900
Moffett Field's Hangar 1 is more than 1,100 feet long, 211 feet tall, and 308 feet wide.

238
00:18:38,901 --> 00:18:44,900
It would be the last of the great steel and concrete airship hangars built in the United

239
00:18:44,901 --> 00:18:47,900
States.

240
00:18:47,901 --> 00:18:53,900
America may have been proud of its fleet of military airships, but in 1930s Europe, airships

241
00:18:53,901 --> 00:18:56,240
Airships were engaged in commercial aviation.

242
00:18:59,320 --> 00:19:03,400
Since 1925, Germany had been allowed to build airships and hangars.

243
00:19:04,460 --> 00:19:12,140
This was possible because Zeppelin Company developer Hugo Ekener had convinced the Allies to ease the terms of the Treaty of Versailles.

244
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:23,460
The Zeppelin Company had flown more than 2,000 intercontinental and continental passenger flights by 1937.

245
00:19:23,900 --> 00:19:31,300
But on May 6th, the beginning of the end of commercial airship aviation took place at

246
00:19:31,301 --> 00:19:36,600
Lakers, just beyond Hangar 1.

247
00:19:36,601 --> 00:19:43,720
On its 11th trip to America, the mammoth 803-foot-long Hindenburg airship burst into flames in front

248
00:19:43,721 --> 00:19:46,220
of film cameras as it attempted to land.

249
00:19:46,221 --> 00:19:48,380
Get this started, get this started.

250
00:19:48,680 --> 00:19:51,340
It's crashing, it's crashing, it's crashing, terrible.

251
00:19:51,800 --> 00:19:53,420
Oh my, get out of the way, please.

252
00:19:53,760 --> 00:19:54,700
It's running, bursting.

253
00:19:54,701 --> 00:19:56,840
Several decades later, there is still debate

254
00:19:56,841 --> 00:19:59,080
on what caused the pride of the German airship fleet

255
00:19:59,081 --> 00:20:02,540
to become completely engulfed in flames in only 34 seconds.

256
00:20:03,200 --> 00:20:06,640
20, hold, 400, 500 feet into...

257
00:20:06,641 --> 00:20:08,360
But undaunted by that tragedy,

258
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:10,180
Germany still went ahead with plans

259
00:20:10,181 --> 00:20:11,680
for a commercial airship center,

260
00:20:12,920 --> 00:20:13,620
Rhein-Main.

261
00:20:16,220 --> 00:20:20,800
The Zeppelin Company envisioned building five Frankfurt-based hangars.

262
00:20:22,060 --> 00:20:25,940
There would be four outlying hangars surrounding one in the middle

263
00:20:25,941 --> 00:20:30,800
that would rotate for takeoffs and landings of airships.

264
00:20:31,360 --> 00:20:35,560
What made Ryan Minen so very unique is that for the first time,

265
00:20:36,060 --> 00:20:41,580
passengers could fly from, say, Holland, from England, from Spain,

266
00:20:42,040 --> 00:20:43,560
into the airport.

267
00:20:43,561 --> 00:20:47,620
They would stay overnight at a very ritzy hotel down in Frankfurt,

268
00:20:47,840 --> 00:20:51,500
and in the next morning, they would be brought out to the airship station

269
00:20:51,501 --> 00:20:55,520
and board their airship for their, quote, intercontinental airship flight.

270
00:20:57,740 --> 00:21:03,080
However, only two of these hangars were built before the Nazis started dismantling the airship program.

271
00:21:05,320 --> 00:21:10,600
The legendary Graf Zeppelin and Graf Zeppelin II, the sister ship of the Hindenburg,

272
00:21:10,601 --> 00:21:15,160
were taken apart and their aluminum skeletons used for airplane production.

273
00:21:17,320 --> 00:21:21,340
On May 5, 1940, both Frankfurt hangars,

274
00:21:21,680 --> 00:21:24,160
shining examples of German innovation and technology,

275
00:21:24,880 --> 00:21:29,100
were dynamited in an effort to ensure the safety of the Rhein-Main airport.

276
00:21:29,920 --> 00:21:33,880
The Nazis felt the massive hangars would be obvious targets for the Allies.

277
00:21:35,860 --> 00:21:37,780
As the U.S. entered World War II,

278
00:21:37,781 --> 00:21:41,780
it rethought its choice of materials used in hangar construction.

279
00:21:42,880 --> 00:21:45,020
Because of its permanence and structural strength,

280
00:21:45,900 --> 00:21:47,660
steel was favored by hangar engineers.

281
00:21:49,160 --> 00:21:52,720
But the use of steel was abandoned because it was needed for the war effort.

282
00:21:53,480 --> 00:21:57,900
So hangars were built of wood, including two more at Moffett Field.

283
00:21:58,980 --> 00:22:03,300
Both Europeans and Americans realized the need to still build large hangars

284
00:22:03,301 --> 00:22:07,460
that could fit a few airships or several blimps inside them simultaneously.

285
00:22:07,780 --> 00:22:13,120
These new wooden hangars would be critical to the success of America's war effort,

286
00:22:13,600 --> 00:22:17,260
because American blimps would play a significant role in World War II.

287
00:22:19,600 --> 00:22:22,660
150 U.S. blimps and airships were in service.

288
00:22:24,200 --> 00:22:28,620
They were used primarily as anti-submarine scouts and in air-sea rescue.

289
00:22:29,620 --> 00:22:33,420
But they also provided invaluable security for the U.S. naval fleet.

290
00:22:33,421 --> 00:22:40,980
Ultimately, United States Navy airships during the Second World War escorted 89,000 surface

291
00:22:40,981 --> 00:22:45,009
ships without a single ship lost to enemy action during the Second World War.

292
00:22:45,010 --> 00:22:45,710
One blimp

293
00:22:45,711 --> 00:22:47,520
is confirmed lost to enemy action.

294
00:22:47,521 --> 00:22:51,360
The operational reliability and record of the U.S. Navy airships

295
00:22:51,361 --> 00:22:56,400
was so successful, in fact, that even after the war, when there was a general cutback

296
00:22:56,401 --> 00:23:02,220
in all aspects of military operation, the airship program was kept alive.

297
00:23:02,221 --> 00:23:07,140
Despite its military achievements, commercial airship use never took flight.

298
00:23:07,141 --> 00:23:12,680
However, commercial aircraft use was growing, creating a demand for a smaller, new kind

299
00:23:12,681 --> 00:23:17,980
of hangar, one that could still be used for military and commercial ventures.

300
00:23:17,981 --> 00:23:24,160
Next, monumental new homes for vehicles that would change the face of aviation.

301
00:23:32,220 --> 00:23:40,120
The Akron air dock is so large that sudden temperature changes cause clouds to form inside the hangar, producing light rainfall.

302
00:23:41,300 --> 00:23:43,460
Hangars will return on Modern Marvels.

303
00:23:46,540 --> 00:23:50,040
In 1966, the mission was clear.

304
00:23:50,720 --> 00:23:52,160
Build the biggest jet ever.

305
00:23:52,420 --> 00:23:58,840
But Boeing engineers knew their existing facilities couldn't house a fleet of jets with a 220-foot wingspan.

306
00:23:59,600 --> 00:24:01,220
A new hangar would be necessary.

307
00:24:02,220 --> 00:24:08,000
The Everett, Washington home for the newly designed Boeing 747 would be massive.

308
00:24:10,400 --> 00:24:16,860
In the spring of 1966, workers started clearing away 780 acres of forest.

309
00:24:17,660 --> 00:24:20,260
Nearly 5 million cubic yards of earth were moved.

310
00:24:21,080 --> 00:24:26,780
That's enough to build a 10-foot-high, 10-foot-wide dike from New York City to Washington, D.C.

311
00:24:26,781 --> 00:24:36,080
34,000 tons of structural steel were acquired for a building to house the airplanes that would change the airline industry

312
00:24:36,081 --> 00:24:42,880
Not only was the jet larger than anything seen before, so too was its hangar

313
00:24:42,881 --> 00:24:49,300
Simply put, it is the largest building by volume in the world

314
00:24:49,301 --> 00:24:53,060
The hangar covers 98 acres of land

315
00:24:53,061 --> 00:24:56,040
And is 114 feet tall

316
00:24:56,041 --> 00:25:03,040
Its front doors are 300 feet wide and 87 feet tall.

317
00:25:05,540 --> 00:25:10,040
The growing popularity of commercial air travel may have led to the enormous Boeing hangar.

318
00:25:10,041 --> 00:25:19,040
But even in its infancy, the aviation industry needed covered shelters to fix, tweak and fine-tune the mode of transportation

319
00:25:19,041 --> 00:25:24,040
that would soon dominate long-distance travel.

320
00:25:24,041 --> 00:25:26,960
At the turn of the 20th century, two Ohio brothers,

321
00:25:27,260 --> 00:25:30,020
Orville and Wilbur Wright, fashioned the first airplane.

322
00:25:31,120 --> 00:25:34,060
But before their historic flight, they faced the same problem

323
00:25:34,061 --> 00:25:37,200
as other lighter-than-air trailblazers from a generation earlier.

324
00:25:38,560 --> 00:25:40,820
Where do you build and then house your vehicle?

325
00:25:43,500 --> 00:25:47,160
That hangar was built at Dayton in about 1902,

326
00:25:47,440 --> 00:25:49,280
and it was just a simple wood-framed building.

327
00:25:50,080 --> 00:25:52,940
Basically, this big garage had to house this machine,

328
00:25:52,941 --> 00:25:57,820
just as, you know, a garage would house a car or a carriage or a buggy of some kind.

329
00:25:59,320 --> 00:26:06,240
This very first airplane hangar was a fraction of the size of von Zeppelin's 425-foot hangar on Lake Constance.

330
00:26:06,600 --> 00:26:09,720
It was roughly 30 feet long and 48 feet wide,

331
00:26:10,240 --> 00:26:14,620
just big enough to protect the Wright brothers' 28-foot long, 40-foot wide plane.

332
00:26:15,600 --> 00:26:18,700
The Wright brothers' success jump-started airplane production,

333
00:26:19,420 --> 00:26:22,360
which, of course, increased the number of airports and hangars.

334
00:26:22,940 --> 00:26:26,300
These early hangars were nothing more than workshops.

335
00:26:27,080 --> 00:26:30,740
They were often just a series of rectangular garages attached to each other

336
00:26:30,741 --> 00:26:33,280
and considerably smaller than their airship siblings.

337
00:26:34,420 --> 00:26:37,960
These early hangars didn't change much until the 1930s.

338
00:26:38,900 --> 00:26:43,820
When we think of airports and aircraft factories and maintenance facilities and hangars,

339
00:26:43,821 --> 00:26:48,340
we think of very utilitarian buildings, steel and glass sheds, basically.

340
00:26:48,341 --> 00:26:56,100
But what's interesting in the 30s is that you have a very conscious attempt to hide the function, if you will, under an elaborate decorative skin.

341
00:26:57,100 --> 00:27:01,060
For the first time, airplane hangars emerged from the shadow of their airship brethren.

342
00:27:01,940 --> 00:27:07,900
These new homes to a rising industry would be elegant structures, not just utilitarian sheds.

343
00:27:08,720 --> 00:27:17,100
Leading the charge were La Bourget in Paris and New York City's LaGuardia, both built as their cities hosted world fairs.

344
00:27:18,340 --> 00:27:22,740
These buildings are very stylistic, very streamlined,

345
00:27:22,741 --> 00:27:25,540
almost classical in some ways with their decorations,

346
00:27:25,541 --> 00:27:30,540
buildings that celebrated flight in a very public way.

347
00:27:30,541 --> 00:27:33,540
However, just a few years later,

348
00:27:33,541 --> 00:27:36,740
the market for beautiful airplane hangars would diminish.

349
00:27:36,741 --> 00:27:40,340
World War II forced designers to build nondescript

350
00:27:40,341 --> 00:27:44,340
utilitarian hangars with just a hint of elegance.

351
00:27:44,341 --> 00:27:49,220
For the first time, arched roofs were used in airplane hanger design.

352
00:27:50,840 --> 00:27:54,520
This extra room allowed hangers to house military blimps during the war.

353
00:27:56,340 --> 00:27:59,200
One of the best examples is in Tustin, California.

354
00:28:00,500 --> 00:28:05,080
There, two World War II-era hangers built of prefabricated, pressure-treated lumber

355
00:28:05,081 --> 00:28:08,320
were completed in an astounding 60 days.

356
00:28:08,321 --> 00:28:14,400
When finished, they were the largest clear-span wooden structures in the world.

357
00:28:15,580 --> 00:28:23,000
Almost 300 feet wide at the base, more than 1,000 feet long, and 171 feet high.

358
00:28:25,020 --> 00:28:30,740
Down the coast in San Diego, the North Island Naval Air Station hangars also had arched roofs.

359
00:28:31,740 --> 00:28:37,280
The hangars were constructed of thin-shell concrete with a span of 289 feet,

360
00:28:37,281 --> 00:28:39,980
and a depth of 240 feet,

361
00:28:40,940 --> 00:28:44,280
making them the largest concrete airplane hangars in the U.S. to date.

362
00:28:47,040 --> 00:28:51,600
For the next 50 years, large airplane hangars dominated the industry.

363
00:28:53,620 --> 00:28:59,180
But the LaGuardia and Le Bourget Art Deco hangars of the 1930s would be remembered.

364
00:29:01,940 --> 00:29:06,020
These elegant hangars captured the attention of the German airliner Lufthansa.

365
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:11,440
In 1994, they bridged the functional role of hangers in remarkable fashion.

366
00:29:13,340 --> 00:29:16,700
This is a very elegant steel and glass building, essentially,

367
00:29:17,300 --> 00:29:21,960
where Lufthansa brings in these big jumbo jets and tears them down,

368
00:29:22,100 --> 00:29:24,600
strips them of the paint, and completely rebuilds them.

369
00:29:24,840 --> 00:29:28,820
What's nice about this is that you have this elegant steel and glass shed,

370
00:29:28,821 --> 00:29:31,780
but you also have a very expressive truss above

371
00:29:31,781 --> 00:29:35,080
to remind you of the origins of these big hangers.

372
00:29:35,081 --> 00:29:42,080
The Lufthansa hangar is 500 feet wide and 90 feet tall.

373
00:29:42,081 --> 00:29:47,080
The entire fleet of jets is remodeled and serviced here.

374
00:29:47,081 --> 00:29:50,080
But the Lufthansa hangar is a rarity.

375
00:29:50,081 --> 00:29:58,080
As an architect or as an architect-engineer team or something, you have to have a client.

376
00:29:58,081 --> 00:30:01,080
You just don't go out and build a hangar.

377
00:30:01,081 --> 00:30:09,320
Unless there is a need, I don't see a great many clients coming forward at this time, maybe in a few years.

378
00:30:10,260 --> 00:30:15,360
The lack of new airplane hangars is a ringing endorsement for the works of yesterday's designers.

379
00:30:17,060 --> 00:30:19,440
They're simply built too well to replace.

380
00:30:22,200 --> 00:30:29,380
Next, America's burgeoning space program erects a structure large enough to house the mightiest rockets ever built.

381
00:30:31,080 --> 00:30:40,700
The Boeing Everett facility is so large that it could house 75 football fields.

382
00:30:41,360 --> 00:30:43,540
Hangers will return on Modern Marvels.

383
00:30:44,780 --> 00:30:52,480
Achieving the goal, before this decade is out, of landing a man on the moon and returning him safely to the Earth.

384
00:30:53,400 --> 00:31:00,660
The space race was on, and the Soviets were in the lead with the successful launch of Sputnik 1, the world's first artificial satellite.

385
00:31:01,080 --> 00:31:10,400
But President Kennedy's 1961 congressional speech energized a nation.

386
00:31:10,401 --> 00:31:24,401
Since 1958, Cape Canaveral, Florida had been the launch site for America's early satellites.

387
00:31:25,680 --> 00:31:29,600
However the existing facilities weren't large enough for the space vehicle that would take

388
00:31:29,601 --> 00:31:31,334
manned to the moon.

389
00:31:31,335 --> 00:31:38,180
This creation would become the largest rocket ever built, the 363-foot-tall

390
00:31:38,181 --> 00:31:42,160
Saturn V, and that rocket would need a hangar unlike any other.

391
00:31:44,280 --> 00:31:47,939
And that's where the vehicle assembly building come in.

392
00:31:47,940 --> 00:31:50,400
The plans started coming out. We kept

393
00:31:50,401 --> 00:31:52,780
looking at the plans, and we didn't believe the plans we were seeing.

394
00:31:52,781 --> 00:32:00,320
The new Saturn V rockets would be built here on Merritt Island, about three miles from Cape Canaveral.

395
00:32:01,760 --> 00:32:08,380
From the start, engineers were concerned about the colossal building possibly sinking into Florida's native marshland.

396
00:32:09,520 --> 00:32:19,540
The solution was to drive 4,200 steel pipe pilings, each 16 inches in diameter, into the marshland to a depth of 160 feet.

397
00:32:19,541 --> 00:32:26,540
This would anchor the Vehicle Assembly Building, or VAB, on a bedrock foundation.

398
00:32:26,541 --> 00:32:32,540
Now that the foundation was in place, workers began to assemble the building.

399
00:32:32,541 --> 00:32:39,540
Most of the materials come in by rail at that time, and a lot of it was assembled just like a rector set.

400
00:32:39,541 --> 00:32:41,856
When you put it in, everything was numbered.

401
00:32:41,857 --> 00:32:45,540
This piece went to that piece, that went to that, and bolted in there.

402
00:32:45,541 --> 00:32:48,540
hoisted it up, you had to have super big cranes there.

403
00:32:52,540 --> 00:32:55,540
Everybody was cutting, welding, molding,

404
00:32:55,541 --> 00:32:58,540
and the things were going up all over the place there.

405
00:32:58,541 --> 00:33:02,540
There was about three different sections of the VAB being built at one time.

406
00:33:02,541 --> 00:33:07,540
There was a continuous upgrading of it as they were trying to design,

407
00:33:07,541 --> 00:33:09,540
because nothing had been built like it.

408
00:33:09,541 --> 00:33:13,540
So it was brand new, and all the engineers would go back and study things,

409
00:33:13,541 --> 00:33:15,920
Next thing, they come back with another modification to it

410
00:33:15,921 --> 00:33:18,100
and add this to it, or delete.

411
00:33:18,500 --> 00:33:20,500
And they had to watch the tonnage of the steel.

412
00:33:22,680 --> 00:33:25,720
The sheer magnitude of the VAB set it apart

413
00:33:25,721 --> 00:33:27,680
from other NASA construction projects.

414
00:33:28,360 --> 00:33:31,760
It would require nearly 100,000 tons of steel

415
00:33:31,761 --> 00:33:34,860
and 65,000 cubic yards of concrete.

416
00:33:36,500 --> 00:33:40,240
When completed, it would be 716 feet long,

417
00:33:40,241 --> 00:33:48,180
518 feet wide, but most impressively, it would be 525 feet high.

418
00:33:48,181 --> 00:33:51,380
It's hard to relate to this building as being very elegant

419
00:33:51,381 --> 00:33:55,780
and being very significant that way in terms of design,

420
00:33:56,000 --> 00:33:58,500
but in scale, it's absolutely overwhelming.

421
00:33:59,060 --> 00:34:04,800
It was built to house four Averna von Braun's massive Saturn V rockets

422
00:34:04,801 --> 00:34:06,880
with Apollo capsules on top.

423
00:34:06,881 --> 00:34:13,180
So that means it's basically four buildings that are more than 34 stories high put together.

424
00:34:13,940 --> 00:34:17,940
In Chicago, I think the average skyscrapers are about 40 stories high.

425
00:34:18,040 --> 00:34:21,660
So think of four of these buildings put together.

426
00:34:22,620 --> 00:34:23,880
This is just incredible.

427
00:34:24,880 --> 00:34:31,440
The VAB would cover eight acres of land and could withstand winds of up to 125 miles an hour.

428
00:34:31,441 --> 00:34:38,560
Despite its massive size, it took only 11 months to finish construction of the facility

429
00:34:38,561 --> 00:34:40,760
that would house the world's largest rockets.

430
00:34:42,460 --> 00:34:47,100
In November 1967, the first Saturn V rocket was launched.

431
00:34:49,680 --> 00:34:56,940
Finally, on July 16, 1969, at 9.32 a.m., the flight that President Kennedy had predicted

432
00:34:56,941 --> 00:34:57,641
began.

433
00:35:01,440 --> 00:35:15,440
The launch site, named to honor the slain ex-president, was the Kennedy Space Center.

434
00:35:20,520 --> 00:35:26,620
Four days later, on July 20, 1969, astronaut Neil Armstrong became the first man to walk

435
00:35:26,621 --> 00:35:27,321
on the moon.

436
00:35:31,440 --> 00:35:37,700
During the 1970s, space exploration reached new heights, as did activity in the VAB.

437
00:35:39,080 --> 00:35:43,700
In 1981, a new era in space exploration dawned.

438
00:35:46,220 --> 00:35:51,080
NASA scientists had developed a new reusable vehicle designed to transport people,

439
00:35:51,440 --> 00:35:54,800
spacecraft, and equipment to and from Earth orbit.

440
00:35:55,220 --> 00:35:59,820
Five, four, we've gone for main engine start, we have main engine start.

441
00:36:01,440 --> 00:36:13,860
The space shuttle, which consists of the orbiter, external fuel tank, and two solid rocket boosters,

442
00:36:14,540 --> 00:36:19,760
is about 184 feet long, 76 feet high, and 78 feet wide.

443
00:36:22,260 --> 00:36:25,880
Like the Saturn V, it would be assembled in the vehicle assembly building.

444
00:36:27,080 --> 00:36:30,600
The VAB underwent a slight facelift to accommodate its new tenant.

445
00:36:31,440 --> 00:36:35,400
They stack the space shuttle in there, they'll bring the orbiter in there,

446
00:36:36,080 --> 00:36:39,680
and then they bring the SRBs, the solid rocket boosters,

447
00:36:40,320 --> 00:36:43,440
and hook them up in there, and then the external tank, they hook it up.

448
00:36:45,380 --> 00:36:48,560
And then they stack it on top of the launch platform,

449
00:36:49,220 --> 00:36:54,460
and then the crawler comes in and picks up the launch platform with the orbiter on top

450
00:36:54,461 --> 00:36:57,640
and walks it out to the launch pad and sets it down on the launch pad.

451
00:36:57,641 --> 00:37:03,960
The Vehicle Assembly Building continues to be the heart of the Kennedy Space Center more

452
00:37:03,961 --> 00:37:07,080
than 40 years after its groundbreaking.

453
00:37:07,081 --> 00:37:17,200
Next, another massive hangar rises at Cape Canaveral to house a new generation of rockets.

454
00:37:17,201 --> 00:37:31,080
The vehicle assembly building's 456-foot doors are taller than both the Statue of Liberty

455
00:37:31,081 --> 00:37:33,000
and St. Peter's Basilica.

456
00:37:35,760 --> 00:37:38,360
Hangars will return on Modern Marvels.

457
00:37:41,040 --> 00:37:44,620
Three decades after the Saturn V carried man to the moon,

458
00:37:47,200 --> 00:37:50,840
Boeing has developed the next generation of rockets.

459
00:37:53,000 --> 00:37:59,480
The Delta IV, a new rocket system that will unlock more mysteries of deep space.

460
00:38:00,060 --> 00:38:05,220
It could actually put a school bus-sized payload into orbit around the Earth.

461
00:38:05,380 --> 00:38:08,480
Or we can launch satellites out past the planets.

462
00:38:09,400 --> 00:38:12,880
And just as the Saturn rockets needed the vehicle assembly building,

463
00:38:12,881 --> 00:38:18,780
the Delta IV family of rockets needed a state-of-the-art hangar.

464
00:38:22,400 --> 00:38:27,480
The SLC-37, which includes the Horizontal Integration Facility,

465
00:38:28,060 --> 00:38:32,480
is the most technologically advanced rocket processing and launch facility in the world.

466
00:38:33,760 --> 00:38:37,660
Here, Delta IV rockets are assembled horizontally instead of vertically.

467
00:38:39,120 --> 00:38:41,840
Because the rockets are closer to the workers' eye level,

468
00:38:42,360 --> 00:38:44,800
More final checks are conducted inside the hangar.

469
00:38:45,560 --> 00:38:48,500
This cuts down on the amount of time a rocket is on the launch pad,

470
00:38:48,620 --> 00:38:50,460
undergoing more pre-launch tests,

471
00:38:50,680 --> 00:38:53,940
which significantly decreases the cost of rocket maintenance.

472
00:38:55,020 --> 00:38:57,740
When we first started developing the Delta IV program,

473
00:38:57,860 --> 00:39:01,100
we were looking for operational items

474
00:39:01,101 --> 00:39:05,180
that could reduce the amount of time it was here at the launch site.

475
00:39:05,640 --> 00:39:09,500
And so one of the things we did was send some of our engineers over to Russia

476
00:39:09,501 --> 00:39:13,780
to see how they did it because they process horizontally,

477
00:39:14,180 --> 00:39:18,340
and then the day before launch, they would erect the vehicle and then shoot it off.

478
00:39:19,040 --> 00:39:22,080
The centerpiece of the new Boeing rocket launch complex

479
00:39:22,081 --> 00:39:26,360
is the aptly named Horizontal Integration Facility, or HIF.

480
00:39:28,220 --> 00:39:32,200
The Horizontal Integration Facility is unique in several ways.

481
00:39:32,760 --> 00:39:37,120
First of all, the bays are 250 feet long and 100 feet wide.

482
00:39:37,560 --> 00:39:40,860
That's in order to handle a heavy vehicle,

483
00:39:40,861 --> 00:39:43,480
which has three common booster cores strapped together.

484
00:39:43,900 --> 00:39:47,040
And so you have to have a very wide hangar.

485
00:39:48,060 --> 00:39:52,680
The 100,000-square-foot, seven-story-tall structure is so wide

486
00:39:52,681 --> 00:39:56,680
that five basketball courts can fit in one of its two bays.

487
00:39:57,900 --> 00:40:02,180
But the stand-alone, jaw-dropping technological marvel of this facility

488
00:40:02,181 --> 00:40:04,580
is its floor.

489
00:40:04,581 --> 00:40:12,860
Because we're mating horizontally, the tolerances are very tight, and so we have to have a super

490
00:40:12,861 --> 00:40:14,800
flat floor.

491
00:40:14,801 --> 00:40:20,340
Now what that means is over that 250 feet by 100 feet, it can only vary 3 eighths of

492
00:40:20,341 --> 00:40:22,580
an inch this way and also this way.

493
00:40:22,581 --> 00:40:29,400
Our engineer stepped up to the plate and came up with a concept of a 12 inch thick floor

494
00:40:29,401 --> 00:40:32,020
with absolutely no reinforcing steel.

495
00:40:32,021 --> 00:40:36,480
And by not having this reinforcing steel, we eliminated construction joints.

496
00:40:37,020 --> 00:40:40,420
So now, with no construction joints, we have a level floor.

497
00:40:40,500 --> 00:40:41,680
In fact, it's a world record.

498
00:40:42,200 --> 00:40:48,100
Engineering News Record magazine recognized that this was the world's levelest floor.

499
00:40:51,600 --> 00:40:56,820
But the world's flattest floor wouldn't stay that way long if the building surrounding it was unstable.

500
00:40:56,821 --> 00:41:03,660
designers encountered the problems faced by engineers charged with erecting the vehicle

501
00:41:03,661 --> 00:41:10,560
assembly building almost 40 years earlier in the same florida marshland however instead of sinking

502
00:41:10,561 --> 00:41:15,900
hundreds of steel pilings into the marshland boeing's need for a super flat floor required

503
00:41:15,901 --> 00:41:24,180
engineers to build six concrete footings each 22 feet wide seven feet deep and 260 feet long

504
00:41:24,181 --> 00:41:29,180
These footings rest on a bed of compacted sand and concrete.

505
00:41:29,181 --> 00:41:34,180
The innovative use of the concrete footings proved a better solution than steel pilings.

506
00:41:34,181 --> 00:41:36,964
Because the footings have more mass than pilings.

507
00:41:36,965 --> 00:41:39,180
That produces a more secure foundation.

508
00:41:39,181 --> 00:41:43,180
Here on the Cape, we were faced with a lot of design challenges,

509
00:41:43,181 --> 00:41:48,180
and one of them being that the wind load is for a 140-mile-an-hour hurricane,

510
00:41:48,181 --> 00:41:52,180
and so the building had to be designed for that.

511
00:41:52,181 --> 00:42:00,220
And so the floors and the foundations are stronger just to hold the building down in those type of winds.

512
00:42:00,920 --> 00:42:07,000
But dealing with the marshland and weather weren't the only obstacles engineers and workers faced while building this hangar.

513
00:42:07,500 --> 00:42:10,800
We had animals to chase out of the project.

514
00:42:11,180 --> 00:42:15,080
We'd have to take a forklift and run wild hogs out of the project almost every morning.

515
00:42:15,081 --> 00:42:19,280
and one time a 10-foot alligator came in through the front door

516
00:42:19,281 --> 00:42:24,000
and one of our pipe fitters was crawling down in the tunnel and stepped on him.

517
00:42:25,320 --> 00:42:26,620
It was quite a surprise.

518
00:42:26,740 --> 00:42:30,440
He was quickly out of the tunnel and we had to call Florida Wildlife

519
00:42:30,441 --> 00:42:32,420
to come and get the alligator out.

520
00:42:34,420 --> 00:42:37,700
In the tradition of engineers of the great early hangars,

521
00:42:37,701 --> 00:42:41,940
the construction company of the HIF paid special attention to the hangars' doors.

522
00:42:41,941 --> 00:42:50,780
But unlike other solid hanger doors, these doors are made out of steel trusses covered with vinyl fabric.

523
00:42:51,920 --> 00:42:55,300
They are 76 feet wide and 44 feet tall.

524
00:42:56,160 --> 00:43:02,720
They can open or close in 55 seconds, and they can withstand 140 mile-per-hour winds.

525
00:43:02,721 --> 00:43:12,280
The HIF may be one of the newest great hangars, but now one of the greatest hangars of a past

526
00:43:12,281 --> 00:43:16,020
age, the Akron AirDoc, looks toward a new life.

527
00:43:16,640 --> 00:43:19,620
We expect in the future to be building airships there again.

528
00:43:20,480 --> 00:43:26,200
We're part of an initiative now to look at high-altitude airships that will be close

529
00:43:26,201 --> 00:43:30,220
in size to the Akron and Macon, although built entirely differently from how they were built.

530
00:43:30,221 --> 00:43:33,920
So we're looking forward to continued long life for the air dock,

531
00:43:34,240 --> 00:43:37,720
and hopefully it'll once again be issuing large airships

532
00:43:37,721 --> 00:43:40,040
for the U.S. military in the not-too-distant future.

533
00:43:42,680 --> 00:43:46,640
The great hangars have all been critical in the development of aviation.

534
00:43:47,780 --> 00:43:52,020
These enormous cathedrals are the silent partners in an ever-evolving venture,

535
00:43:53,680 --> 00:43:57,300
extending man's reach into the skies and outer space.

